This is a solid RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) architecture. Transitioning this from the AWS/Kubernetes-heavy stack in your image to a native Azure environment allows you to leverage more managed services, which simplifies the "Multi-Sub Agents" and "Monitoring" layers.
Here is how the components map from your diagram to the Azure ecosystem:
Azure RAG Architecture Mapping
| Function | Original Component (AWS/OSS) | Azure Equivalent |
|---|---|---|
| Data Sources | Amazon S3 / RDBMS | Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS Gen2) / Azure SQL |
| Data Ingestion | Chunking, Embedding, Graph Ext. | Azure Data Factory / Azure AI Search (Integrated Vectorization) |
| Vector DB | Qdrant / Neo4j | Azure AI Search (Vector Store) / Azure Cosmos DB (for Apache Gremlin) |
| Orchestration | Multi-Sub Agents (Planner) | Semantic Kernel / LangChain on Azure Functions |
| Compute / LLMs | Ray Serve / vLLM | Azure OpenAI Service / Azure Machine Learning (Online Endpoints) |
| API Services | API Gateway / Auth | Azure API Management (APIM) / Entra ID |
| Deployment | EKS / Helm / Terraform | Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) / Bicep / Terraform |
| Monitoring | Prometheus / Grafana | Azure Monitor / Application Insights |
The Proposed Azure Architecture
1. The Data & Ingestion Layer
Instead of manual "Graph Extraction" scripts, use Azure AI Search. It now handles the automated "cracking" of documents, chunking, and embedding in a single pipeline.
 * Storage: Use Azure Blob Storage for raw docs.
 * Processing: Azure AI Search Indexers can automatically convert these into vectors.
2. The Intelligence Layer (Agents & LLMs)
 * The Models: Replace self-hosted vLLM with Azure OpenAI Service. It provides the same GPT-4o models but with enterprise-grade security and private networking.
 * The Agents: Use Semantic Kernel (Microsoft’s native framework) to build the "Planner" and "Multi-Sub Agents." These can be hosted as Azure Functions for a serverless, cost-effective approach.
3. The API & Security Gate
 * Gateway: Use Azure API Management (APIM). It specifically has "circuit breaker" and "token rate limiting" policies designed for LLMs.
 * Auth: Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD) handles the "Auth & Security" block from your diagram, providing Managed Identities so services can talk without hardcoded passwords.
4. Monitoring & Evaluation
 * Replace Prometheus/Grafana with Azure AI Studio's Evaluation tools.
 * Use Application Insights to track "Groundness," "Relevance," and "Coherence" metrics for your RAG bot in real-time.
Comparison Summary
 * Why Azure over the original? The original relies heavily on managing your own clusters (Ray Serve, Qdrant). In Azure, you shift to PaaS (Platform as a Service), which means less time managing infrastructure and more time tuning your Agent's "Planner" logic.
